        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Pure sine wave reproduces the smooth curve your appliances were designed around. Modified sine wave approximates it with abrupt steps, and those steps become heat in motors, buzz in transformers and confusion in anything with electronic controls.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            On modern power stations this is a solved problem — essentially all of them are pure sine wave, and the specification is worth confirming rather than agonising over. The real risk lies in cheap car inverters and unbranded units, where the waveform is often modified and rarely advertised.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The test is simple. If the appliance is a heating element or a filament, waveform does not matter. If it contains a motor, a transformer, a control board or a medical label, buy pure sine wave and stop thinking about it.
          </p>
